<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n58974" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κόππα</span></b> , τό,
		= Hebr. &lt;*&gt; (<u>Koph</u>), a letter
		(&lt;*&gt;) standing between <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">π</span> and
			<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ρ</span> in early Greek alphabets, <i><span class="en">
			<u>IG</u>
			14.2420
		</span></i>, etc.; later displaced by <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κ</span>, but
		surviving in Latin as Q and retained in Greek as a numeral =
		<i><span class="en">
			90
		</span></i>, e.g. <i><span class="en">
			<u>PSI</u>
			8.958.24
		</span></i>
		<span class="entity">(iv A. D.)</span>: prov., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b>
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">οὐδὲ κόππα γιγνώσκων</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Parmeno
					1
				</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Greek-English Lexicon</u>
        </u> (Henry George Liddell, Robert Scott, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1940) </div>
